 ## Overview
-GazeNeRF is a 3D-aware gaze redirection method that reformulates the task as a neural volume rendering problem [S1]. It uses a two-stream MLP architecture to separately predict volumetric features for the face and eye regions, allowing for rigid 3D rotation of the eye features to achieve gaze redirection while preserving identity [S1]. It differs from 2D methods (e.g., STED, FAZE) by explicitly modeling the 3D nature of eyeball rotation and face deformation via separate volumetric streams, whereas 2D methods apply weak rotation priors to mixed 2D latent features [S1]. It differs from EyeNeRF by focusing on gaze redirection accuracy and using disentangled feature streams with explicit 3D rotation, whereas EyeNeRF focuses on high-fidelity rendering and relighting quality [S1].
+GazeNeRF is a 3D-aware method for gaze redirection that reformulates the task as a neural volume rendering problem, addressing the limitation of existing 2D methods that fail to generate 3D-consistent results [S1]. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [PI-NeRF](12124-pi-nerf.md) and [NeRF2Points](12125-nerf2points.md) by employing a two-stream MLP architecture that separately predicts volumetric features for the face and eye regions, allowing for explicit rigid 3D rotation of the eye features to achieve gaze redirection while preserving identity [S1]. Unlike [HeadNeRF](11313-model-architecture-modifications.md) which uses a single MLP conditioned on gaze labels, GazeNeRF disentangles the eyes from the rest of the face to model their independent 3D movements [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:**
-    *   3D spatial position $x \in \mathbb{R}^3$.
-    *   View direction $d \in \mathbb{R}^3$.
-    *   Gaze label $g \in \mathbb{R}^2$, a 2D vector consisting of pitch and yaw angles of the gaze vector in radians [S1].
-    *   Latent codes $z_{sh}, z_{ex}, z_{te}, z_{il} \in \mathbb{R}^k$ representing shape, expression, texture (albedo), and illumination, respectively. These are initialized from 3DMM parameters [S1].
-    *   Reference image (for supervision during training) [S1].
-*   **Outputs:**
-    *   Rendered 2D images: $I_{wf}$ (whole face), $I_{fw/o}$ (face without eyes), and $I_e$ (eyes only) [S1].
+*   **Inputs:** A reference face image (resized to $512 \times 512$ pixels), a target gaze label $g$ (2D vector of pitch and yaw angles in radians), and learnable latent codes $z^*$ for shape ($z_{sh}$), expression ($z_{ex}$), texture ($z_{te}$), and illumination ($z_{il}$) initialized from 3DMM parameters [S1].
+*   **Outputs:** A rendered 2D image $I_{wf}$ of the whole face with redirected gaze, along with intermediate feature maps and images for the face-only region ($I_{fw/o}$) and eyes region ($I_e$) [S1].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Two-stream MLPs:**
-    *   $h_{\theta_{fw/o}}$: Face-only stream parameters $\theta_{fw/o}$.
-    *   $h_{\theta_{e}}$: Eye stream parameters $\theta_{e}$ [S1].
-*   **Volume Feature Maps:**
-    *   $F_{fw/o} \in \mathbb{R}^{64 \times 64 \times 258}$: Low-resolution volume feature map for the face region [S1].
-    *   $F_{e} \in \mathbb{R}^{64 \times 64 \times 258}$: Low-resolution volume feature map for the eye region [S1].
-*   **Rotated Eye Feature Map:**
-    *   $F_{e-rot} \in \mathbb{R}^{64 \times 64 \times 86 \times 3}$: Reshaped version of $F_e$ to allow 3D rotation matrix multiplication [S1].
-*   **Merged Feature Map:**
-    *   $F_{wf} \in \mathbb{R}^{64 \times 64 \times 258}$: Merged feature map for the whole face [S1].
-*   **Masks:**
-    *   $M_{wf}$: Whole face mask.
-    *   $M_f$: Face-only mask.
-    *   $M_e$: Eyes mask [S1].
+*   **Two-stream MLPs:** Two separate Multi-Layer Perceptrons, $h_{\theta_{fw/o}}$ for the face-only region and $h_{\theta_{e}}$ for the eyes region, with learnable parameters $\theta_{fw/o}$ and $\theta_{e}$ respectively [S1].
+*   **Volume Feature Maps:** Low-resolution feature maps $F_{fw/o}$ and $F_e$ with dimensions $\mathbb{R}^{64 \times 64 \times 258}$ generated by volume rendering the MLP outputs [S1].
+*   **Rotated Eye Feature Map:** $F_e$ is reshaped to $\mathbb{R}^{64 \times 64 \times 86 \times 3}$ to facilitate 3D rotation matrix multiplication [S1].
+*   **Masks:** Binary masks $M_{wf}$ (whole face), $M_f$ (face-only), and $M_e$ (eyes) generated via face parsing models to supervise specific regions [S1].
 
