 ## Background
-GazeNeRF was proposed in 2023 by the authors of "GazeNeRF: 3D-Aware Gaze Redirection with Neural Radiance Fields" [S1]. It builds on the intuition that the face region and eyeballs are separate 3D structures that move in a coordinated yet independent fashion [S1]. It shares similarities with EyeNeRF but focuses on gaze redirection accuracy rather than high-fidelity rendering and relighting quality [S1]. It differs from HeadNeRF, which uses a single MLP conditioned on gaze labels, by using explicit 3D rotation on separated eye features [S1]. It is used for gaze redirection tasks, outperforming 2D methods like STED and FAZE which lack 3D awareness [S1].
+GazeNeRF was proposed in 2023 by authors who contributed equally to the work, published in the CVPR proceedings [S1]. It builds on the intuition that the face region and eyeballs are separate 3D structures that move in a coordinated yet independent fashion. The method leverages advancements in conditional image-based neural radiance fields and proposes a two-stream architecture to predict volumetric features for the face and eye regions separately [S1]. It differs from 2D methods like STED and FAZE, which apply weak rotation priors to mixed 2D latent features, and from EyeNeRF, which focuses on high-fidelity rendering rather than gaze redirection accuracy [S1].
 
